:: Day5_Afternoon_B [day5 afternoon downtown nicolette salon]
<<nobr>>
<<centerImage "image/events/mall.png">>
<<set $currentLocation to "events/mall">>

<p>The mall renovation isn't done yet. Half the storefronts are still covered in paper and plastic wrap, their future identities hidden behind construction barriers and "Coming Soon" signs that promise coffee shops and clothing stores and the homogenized retail experience that all malls eventually converge toward.</p>
<p>But the salon is open. Tucked into a corner of the ground floor near the east entrance, its glass front blazing with light, it stands out from the construction zone like a diamond in a dumpster. "Velvet Shears" says the sign, in a cursive font that's trying very hard to be sophisticated. The interior is all clean lines and chrome and mirrors that multiply everything by infinity.</p>
<p>I walk in because the door is open and the air conditioning is on and the heat outside is the kind that makes your shirt stick to your back and your brain stick to stupid decisions.</p>
<p>Nicolette is behind the counter. I see her before she sees me and the two seconds of unobserved observation give me time to take her in without the self-consciousness that comes from being watched back.</p>
<p>Platinum pixie cut. Short, textured, styled with the kind of deliberate messiness that takes an hour to achieve. Tattoos crawling up both arms, sleeves of ink that tell stories I can't read from this distance, flowers and skulls and geometric patterns weaving together in a tapestry that covers her from wrist to shoulder. Her outfit defies logic. A cropped vintage band tee over a pleated skirt over combat boots, accessories that clash and coordinate simultaneously, the whole ensemble held together by sheer confidence and the gravitational pull of someone who has never once in her life dressed for anyone else's approval.</p>
<p>She's magnetic. Not beautiful in the conventional sense, not in the way that Chloe is beautiful or Sophia is beautiful, but arresting. You look at Nicolette and your eyes don't want to leave.</p>
<p>She looks up. Sees me.</p>
<p>"Need a haircut, handsome?" The word comes out casual, automatic, the way servers say "can I help you" and doctors say "what brings you in today." But her eyes are already doing something else. They're assessing. Measuring. Running calculations that have nothing to do with small talk.</p>
<p>She comes around the counter. Gets close. Closer than strangers usually get. Her fingers are in my hair before I've consented to them being there, running through from the crown to the nape, testing the texture, the weight, the fall.</p>
<p>"You've got great bone structure," she says. Her fingers trace my jawline. Not a caress. An evaluation. "And this hair. A little long on top. Some texture work, maybe some highlights... you'd look incredible."</p>
<p>Her fingers in my hair feel different from every other touch I've received this week. Vanessa touches to correct. Evelyn touches to control. Chloe touches to possess. Sophia touches to comfort. Nicolette touches to create. I can feel the hairdresser in her fingertips, the artist assessing her canvas, and the canvas she's assessing is my face and my head and the raw material of whoever I might become if I sit in her chair and let her work.</p>
<p>"I'm just browsing," I manage.</p>
<p>"Browsing." She grins. "That's what they all say. Then they sit down and three hours later they're walking out looking like a completely different person and loving every second of it." She pulls a card from behind the counter. Holds it out between two fingers.</p>
<p>The card says "Nicolette | Velvet Shears" in the same cursive as the sign. On the back, a list of services: Haircuts. Color. Manicures. Pedicures. Waxing. Makeup Application.</p>
<p>Makeup Application.</p>
<p>The last one catches my eye and holds it and my eyes stay on those two words for a beat too long and Nicolette sees me looking and her grin widens by exactly one millimeter.</p>
<p>"When you're ready," she says. The wink she gives me is not subtle. "I'm here every day except Monday. And I never forget a face. Especially one with this much potential."</p>
<<set $hasNicolettesCard to true>>
<<addItem "Nicolette's business card">>
<p>I pocket the card.<<if $jamalPreIntro or $zaraFlyer>> It sits in my jeans against <<if $jamalPreIntro>>Jamal's personal training card<</if>><<if $jamalPreIntro and $zaraFlyer>> and <</if>><<if $zaraFlyer>>the Pulse Nightclub flyer<</if>>, a growing collection of invitations to places I haven't been yet but know I'll end up.<<else>> It sits in my jeans, my first invitation to a place I haven't been yet but know I'll end up.<</if>> <<if $jamalPreIntro and $zaraFlyer>>The salon. The gym. The club. Three doors I haven't walked through. Three versions of Alex waiting on the other side.<<else>>The salon. A door I haven't walked through. A version of Alex waiting on the other side.<</if>></p>
<p>"See you around, handsome." She's already turning back to the counter. "Or should I say, see you around, gorgeous. Either way, you know where to find me."</p>
<p>I leave the mall with Nicolette's card burning in my pocket<<if $jamalPreIntro or $zaraFlyer>> like all the other cards and flyers that burn in all my other pockets<</if>>. This city is full of women who see me and decide I'm a project. Vanessa at the cafe. Evelyn at home. Chloe in her bedroom. Sophia in her garden. Now Nicolette behind her counter with her tattoos and her platinum hair and her fingers in my hair and her list of services that ends with Makeup Application.</p>
<p>The mall opens properly on Day 8. Nicolette will remember me. She'll already have ideas.</p>
<p>The question isn't whether I'll go back. The question is what I'll look like when I leave.</p>

<<statChange "feminization" 1>>
<<statChange "embarrassment" 1>>
<<set $nicoletteRelationship += 3>>
<<set $nicoletteSalonPreviewed to true>>
<<set $nicoletteMakeupCardSeen to true>>
<<set $day5_nicolettePreviewDone to true>>
<<spendTime 1>>
